{
 "id": "2022-zhang-forest",
 "name": "Forest Recreation Space Cognition: Six Liaoning Forest Spaces, Ten 7-Point Indicators",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Zhang et al. (2022), Forests 13:136, \"Psychological Cognitive Factors Affecting Visual Behavior and Satisfaction Preference for Forest Recreation Space\". Participants first look at six photographs of forest recreation spaces in Liaoning forest parks, northern China (dynamic water, static water, lookout, broadleaf, coniferous and mixed forest), 15 s each. They then see each photograph again and rate it on ten indicators from 1 (worst) to 7 (best): plant species diversity, changing landscape content, three-dimensional near-middle landscape, rich color, bright color, open space, visible distant landscape, neat space, sense of hierarchy and overall satisfaction. Background questions cover gender, age, study level and field of study (the paper compares landscape and non-landscape majors). The six photographs are the paper's own stimuli, cropped from Figure 2 (CC BY 4.0, photographed by the authors). They are used with credit and the template is taken down if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) the eye-tracking recording (SMI glasses, projector screen) is not included; only the viewing phase is kept. (2) The length of the five blank screens between photographs is not reported; the template uses 6 s, which fits the reported ~120 s viewing time. (3) The order of photographs is not reported; the template uses the order of Figure 2 in both parts. (4) In the paper the questionnaire was filled in afterwards in a second laboratory; here it follows online right after viewing. (5) The paper recruited Shenyang Agricultural University students aged 18-24; the template is open to anyone and asks for age and field of study.",
